title: attach to an already-exited container never closes on Podman - hangs forever
updated: 2026-08-27T17:20:57Z
---

Measured on rootless Podman 5.4.2 (API 1.41), 2026-08-27, while capturing the
karr #36 fixtures.

POST /containers/{id}/attach?stream=1&stdout=1&stderr=1&logs=1 against a
container that has ALREADY EXITED answers 200 with
Content-Type: application/vnd.docker.raw-stream, no Content-Length and no
chunked encoding -- and then never closes the connection. Confirmed with
curl -v. The buffered frames do arrive; the socket then just hangs.

It does not close even when the client sends `Connection: close`, which
_request always does.

Attaching to a container that is still RUNNING is fine: the stream closes
naturally when the container exits, and those bytes are byte-identical to
GET /containers/{id}/logs on the same engine (that was #36's measurement).

Consequence for this distribution: containers->attach on a stopped container
is an unbounded hang, not an error and not an empty result. It is the same
family as the documented streaming hazard, but sharper -- here there is no
"bound the window" option, because the daemon has no more data to send and
still will not hang up. A callback does not help either: nothing will ever
call $stop->().

That is why attach is deliberately absent from the live subtests in
t/containers_endpoints.t, which is now recorded as a comment there.

To decide, none of it done:
  - whether attach should refuse (or warn) when inspect says the container
    is not running -- costs an extra round trip on every call
  - whether the transport wants a read timeout at all, which is the general
    version of this problem and touches Role::HTTP
  - whether this is Podman-specific: NOT measured against Docker, there is
    no Docker on this machine.

Related: #45 (Podman-shaped error inside a 200), #50 (engine-dependent croak
text). All three are the same underlying theme -- this client trusts a
well-behaved daemon in places where Podman is not one.
